A/B measurement of the non-SI page-lock release (recover_deadlock_sync_dta).

This is a reporting test, not a pass/fail one.  It always exits 0 unless the
harness itself breaks; read the numbers it prints.

Purpose
-------

recover_deadlock_sync_dta controls whether a non-SI read releases the page
locks it holds when another transaction is found waiting on them, instead of
holding them until the read finishes.  The expectation is that a blocked
writer gets through sooner with the feature on, at some cost to the reader
that gave up its locks and has to reposition.

The test runs the reco-ddlk-sql workload twice against the same database --
once with the tunable off, once with it on -- and prints a per-phase
comparison so the two arms can be read side by side.  The tunable is set on
every node of the cluster, since the readers connect via 'default' and spread
across the replicants.

The workload is 16 reader threads continuously scanning a 3-way join while a
single writer thread rewrites the same rows underneath them.  Table a holds 10
rows (one per a1), b holds 1000 (100 per b1, b1 in 1..10) and c holds 100000
(1000 per c1, c1 in 1..100).  Each key column is indexed INCLUDE ALL, and the
readers join on exactly those columns, so reader and writer contend on the
same index pages.

One write statement therefore updates or deletes 1, 100 or 1000 rows in a
single transaction, holding write locks on all of them plus their index
entries until it commits.  The c statements are the ones that lose to
contention; the 100-row and 1-row ones do not.  Note the writer only walks key
values 1..10, so it touches all of a and b but only a tenth of c.

Parameters
----------

Set these on the make command line, the same way as CLUSTER:

  RECO_DDLK_PERF_RUNS=<N>          Number of off/on cycles.  Default 1.  With
                                   N > 1 the report gains a table averaging
                                   every run, ahead of the per-run tables.

  RECO_DDLK_PERF_FIRST_ARM=off|on  Which arm runs first in each cycle.
                                   Default off.  Useful for checking that a
                                   result is not an artifact of arm ordering
                                   (the first arm runs against a colder cache).

  RECO_DDLK_PERF_LOCKSTATS=0|1     Collect the server-side lock-wait and
                                   lock-release counters.  Default 0.  With 1
                                   the report gains the lk_ columns described
                                   below; with 0 neither the tunable nor the
                                   columns appear.  Raise TEST_TIMEOUT when
                                   turning it on -- a single-node run measured
                                   13m off and 17.5m on, over the 15m default:

                                     TEST_TIMEOUT=30m RECO_DDLK_PERF_LOCKSTATS=1 make reco-ddlk-perf

Default runs measured 7.5 to 12 minutes single-node, so the timeout is 15m and
the test is part of the normal sweep.  Run it on its own with:

  make reco-ddlk-perf

Raise the timeout when asking for more cycles, since RECO_DDLK_PERF_RUNS=N
multiplies the runtime by N:

  TEST_TIMEOUT=120m RECO_DDLK_PERF_RUNS=4 make reco-ddlk-perf

Keeping the value in this Makefile below 120m is also what keeps the test in
$(ALL_TESTS): tools/get_tests_inorder.sh greps the Makefile for TEST_TIMEOUT
and drops anything at or above 120.  It reads the file, not the environment,
so overriding TEST_TIMEOUT on the command line changes only how long the run
is allowed to take -- it does not remove the test from the sweep.

Output
------

Everything goes to stdout, so it lands in the test's .testcase log.  Per arm
the raw tool output is kept in the test directory as arm_off_run<N>.out and
arm_on_run<N>.out.

The report is a metric legend followed by an off / on / % diff table for the
update phase and for the delete phase:

  phase_ms     wall-clock duration of the phase
  wr_count     writer statements completed
  wr_avg_ms    average writer statement latency
  wr_max_ms    slowest single writer statement
  rd_scans     reader scans completed, summed over the 16 reader threads
  rd_avg_ms    average reader scan latency over those threads
  rd_min_ms    fastest single reader scan
  rd_max_ms    slowest single reader scan
  data_errors  soft errors so far (see below)
  % diff       (on - off) / off * 100

Lock counters
-------------

RECO_DDLK_PERF_LOCKSTATS=1 turns on the server's lock_instrumentation tunable
and adds a second block of columns, summed over every node because readers and
writers block on different ones:

  lk_rd_wait_us    lock wait by SQL read cursors
  lk_wr_wait_us    lock wait by SQL write transactions
  lk_other_wait_us lock wait by neither -- replication apply, utilities.  On a
                   cluster this is where the write-side contention lands, since
                   SQL does not run on the master
  lk_rel_us        total time inside recover_deadlock, all callers, split into
                   lk_rel_unlock_us (dropping the cursor page locks),
                   lk_rel_sleep_us (deliberate poll() backoff),
                   lk_rel_reacq_us (waiting to get the locks back) and
                   lk_rel_reval_us (cursor revalidation)
  lk_sync_us       pre-release data cursor and blob sync, which runs only with
                   the feature on and is therefore its added reader cost

Each has a matching _waits or _count column.  Only lk_rel_reacq_us is a lock
wait; lk_rel_sleep_us in particular is backoff, and in practice dominates
lk_rel_us, so subtract it before reading lk_rel_us as overhead.

It is off by default because the counters are not free and the point of the
test is to compare two arms under otherwise stock conditions.  Turn it on when
you want to know where the time went rather than how much of it there was.

Reading the two phases
----------------------

They stop for different reasons, so the same column does not mean the same
thing in both:

  update is time-bounded.  It repeats a 30-statement iteration until 60s have
  elapsed, checking the clock only at iteration boundaries.  wr_count is thus
  always a multiple of 30 and is the throughput signal -- faster writes fit
  more iterations into the window.  phase_ms is a poor metric here: the phase
  cannot finish early, and its value is set by how far the last iteration
  overshoots 60s, which one stalled statement can dominate.

  delete is work-bounded.  One pass deletes each key value once and stops, so
  wr_count is always 30 and carries no signal, and phase_ms measures fixed
  work.  This is the cleaner of the two speed comparisons.

data_errors is misleadingly named.  It counts a statement that failed outright
as well as a genuine data mismatch, and in practice what shows up is the
former: a 1000-row UPDATE on c abandoned after exhausting its 500 block
transaction retries against the readers, returned to the client as
CDB2ERR_DEADLOCK (203).  A non-zero value is not by itself a correctness
problem -- check the arm's raw output to see which kind it was.  The counter
is cumulative over the run and is not reset per phase, so a later phase's
value includes earlier phases'.

The workload is run with -p (perf mode) precisely so that such a failure is
counted and the arm keeps going; without it one lost statement would kill the
arm before any numbers came out.

Requirements
------------

Needs the reco-ddlk-sql tool.  A plain `make comdb2` does not build it:

  cmake --build <builddir> --target reco-ddlk-sql -j$(nproc)

The test checks for it up front and fails with that hint rather than
producing an empty table.

Runs on a single node or on a cluster.  Note the two are not comparable:
single-node puts all 16 readers and the writer in one process sharing one lock
table, which produces far more contention than a cluster, where SQL runs on
the replicants and writes commit on the master.
